WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.384
Welcome to Module 3 of the
Provider Notices learning path:

2
00:00:04.384 --> 00:00:06.912
the Microsoft 365 Message center.

3
00:00:06.912 --> 00:00:10.411
This is Gate 2 of the provider
notices implementation guide,

4
00:00:10.411 --> 00:00:13.350
with the second chapter of
the developer workbook.

5
00:00:13.350 --> 00:00:18.596
The Message center is where Microsoft
announces changes, retirements and actions

6
00:00:18.596 --> 00:00:23.538
for a Microsoft 365 tenant, and service
health lists incidents and advisories.

7
00:00:23.538 --> 00:00:27.532
Microsoft Graph's service
communications A P I reads both,

8
00:00:27.532 --> 00:00:29.363
with two read permissions.

9
00:00:29.363 --> 00:00:32.942
In this module you read the
fixture tenant's posts and issues,

10
00:00:32.942 --> 00:00:37.237
see which ones Microsoft said need
action and which the kit's rule marked,

11
00:00:37.237 --> 00:00:41.675
and watch the kit refuse to follow a next
link off the tenant's own Graph host.

12
00:00:41.675 --> 00:00:46.900
It takes about fifty minutes, with a lab
on fixtures and a lab for a test tenant.

13
00:00:46.900 --> 00:00:52.760
The code is illustrative: tested against
fixtures, not run against a live tenant.

14
00:00:56.510 --> 00:00:59.710
Why does Microsoft 365 need its own gate?

15
00:00:59.710 --> 00:01:04.522
In the admin center, each administrator's
view of the Message center is their own.

16
00:01:04.522 --> 00:01:08.709
Archiving a post removes it only
from that administrator's view,

17
00:01:08.709 --> 00:01:13.922
and the weekly digest goes, by default, to
each administrator's own primary email.

18
00:01:13.922 --> 00:01:18.779
Microsoft gives at least thirty days'
notice of a major update that needs action,

19
00:01:18.779 --> 00:01:21.723
and the act-by date is
rare enough that Microsoft

20
00:01:21.723 --> 00:01:24.372
says to pay extra
attention when it appears.

21
00:01:24.372 --> 00:01:28.270
So the program reads the posts
from Microsoft Graph instead,

22
00:01:28.270 --> 00:01:32.635
through the service communications
A P I, with two read permissions.

23
00:01:32.635 --> 00:01:33.647
There's a trap.

24
00:01:33.647 --> 00:01:38.391
A GCC High tenant read on the
worldwide Graph host returns nothing,

25
00:01:38.391 --> 00:01:41.236
and an empty list looks like a quiet week.

26
00:01:41.236 --> 00:01:44.188
So the kit reads only the
cloud the settings name,

27
00:01:44.188 --> 00:01:47.950
and refuses a session in
another environment or tenant.

28
00:01:51.700 --> 00:01:54.613
Here's what you'll be able to
do by the end of this module.

29
00:01:54.613 --> 00:01:57.623
You'll name the two Graph
permissions the reader needs,

30
00:01:57.623 --> 00:02:00.099
and the role that data
privacy messages need.

31
00:02:00.099 --> 00:02:04.312
You'll say which Graph host
each Microsoft 365 cloud uses,

32
00:02:04.312 --> 00:02:07.000
and why the wrong one fails silently.

33
00:02:07.000 --> 00:02:11.350
You'll map Message center posts and service
health issues to the record's kinds.

34
00:02:11.350 --> 00:02:13.719
You'll tell a date Microsoft set from the

35
00:02:13.719 --> 00:02:16.087
kit's rule for a
prevent-or-fix-issue post.

36
00:02:16.087 --> 00:02:19.876
And you'll show that the kit
refuses a next link on another host.

37
00:02:19.876 --> 00:02:23.807
You need Module 1, for the record
and its action basis, and the intake,

38
00:02:23.807 --> 00:02:28.381
triage and routing chapter of Compliance
Automation and Day-Two Operations,

39
00:02:28.381 --> 00:02:32.313
which explains why nothing is
approved on the strength of an email.

40
00:02:32.313 --> 00:02:36.475
Lab 1 needs PowerShell 7.1
or later with Pester 5.

41
00:02:36.475 --> 00:02:37.770
No tenant.

42
00:02:41.520 --> 00:02:44.020
Unit 2 starts with what
Microsoft gives you.

43
00:02:44.020 --> 00:02:48.973
Learn's service communications overview
names three list calls on Graph version one

44
00:02:48.973 --> 00:02:53.577
point oh: messages, for Message center
posts, which need Service Message dot Read

45
00:02:53.577 --> 00:02:55.879
dot All; and issues and health overviews,

46
00:02:55.879 --> 00:02:58.320
which need Service
Health dot Read dot All.

47
00:02:58.320 --> 00:03:02.251
The kit keeps the health overviews
in the raw record for the run;

48
00:03:02.251 --> 00:03:04.670
the notices come from posts and issues.

49
00:03:04.670 --> 00:03:08.032
Both permissions are granted
only by an administrator,

50
00:03:08.032 --> 00:03:10.883
and a delegated sign-in
also needs an admin role.

51
00:03:10.883 --> 00:03:15.282
One exception matters: data privacy
messages are visible only to Global

52
00:03:15.282 --> 00:03:18.509
Administrators and Message
Center Privacy Readers,

53
00:03:18.509 --> 00:03:21.808
so where they matter, name a
privacy reader to read them.

54
00:03:21.808 --> 00:03:26.669
And Learn has its own cmdlet for posts,
Get M g Service Announcement Message.

55
00:03:26.669 --> 00:03:31.620
It's the interim path before the reader
exists, and Lab 2's cross-check.

56
00:03:35.370 --> 00:03:39.857
Each Microsoft 365 cloud
has its own Graph host.

57
00:03:39.857 --> 00:03:44.170
GCC keeps the worldwide endpoint,
graph dot microsoft dot com,

58
00:03:44.170 --> 00:03:47.045
and signs in with the Global environment.

59
00:03:47.045 --> 00:03:48.895
That's the workbook's baseline.

60
00:03:48.895 --> 00:03:54.204
As a side note, GCC High uses graph
dot microsoft dot U S, and DoD uses

61
00:03:54.204 --> 00:03:59.907
dod dash graph dot microsoft dot U S,
each with its own environment name.

62
00:03:59.907 --> 00:04:03.153
The A P I is available
on the national clouds,

63
00:04:03.153 --> 00:04:07.932
and each list method's table shows
U S Government L four and L five.

64
00:04:07.932 --> 00:04:09.507
The kit has two guards.

65
00:04:09.507 --> 00:04:12.623
Before the first call, it
compares the Graph session's

66
00:04:12.623 --> 00:04:16.446
environment and tenant with the
settings, and refuses a mismatch.

67
00:04:16.446 --> 00:04:19.174
And a next link is a U
R L the response chose,

68
00:04:19.174 --> 00:04:23.107
so the kit follows it only if it's
on the cloud's own Graph host.

69
00:04:23.107 --> 00:04:24.759
Anything else is refused,

70
00:04:24.759 --> 00:04:28.900
so the token never goes somewhere
the agency didn't choose.

71
00:04:32.650 --> 00:04:37.838
No post category says retirement, so
the kit maps posts and issues to kinds.

72
00:04:37.838 --> 00:04:40.375
A post tagged Retirement is a retirement.

73
00:04:40.375 --> 00:04:44.050
Plan-for-change and
prevent-or-fix-issue posts are changes.

74
00:04:44.050 --> 00:04:46.425
Stay-informed posts are advisories.

75
00:04:46.425 --> 00:04:49.754
A service health issue is
an incident or an advisory,

76
00:04:49.754 --> 00:04:53.237
by its classification, and it
closes when it's resolved.

77
00:04:53.237 --> 00:04:57.957
Action required comes from one field:
Microsoft's action-required-by date,

78
00:04:57.957 --> 00:05:02.987
the only field that says Microsoft wants
action, and the record's basis names it.

79
00:05:02.987 --> 00:05:07.248
The one kit rule is for a
prevent-or-fix-issue post without a date:

80
00:05:07.248 --> 00:05:10.362
it's action required, with
no date, labeled rule.

81
00:05:10.362 --> 00:05:12.175
No date is ever made up.

82
00:05:12.175 --> 00:05:15.783
The kit also matches enum
values without regard to case,

83
00:05:15.783 --> 00:05:20.961
strips the markup from a post's body, caps
the summary at two thousand characters,

84
00:05:20.961 --> 00:05:23.863
and closes a post once
its end date has passed.

85
00:05:23.863 --> 00:05:29.660
One gap remains: Learn doesn't enumerate
the tags, so that's not yet specified.

86
00:05:33.410 --> 00:05:34.135
Try it.

87
00:05:34.135 --> 00:05:38.012
Lab 1 runs in the provider
notices folder of the examples,

88
00:05:38.012 --> 00:05:41.394
in PowerShell 7, against a
fictional tenant in G C C.

89
00:05:41.394 --> 00:05:46.674
Step one lists what Microsoft 365 needs:
three calls, and only two permissions,

90
00:05:46.674 --> 00:05:49.808
Service Message dot Read
dot All for the posts,

91
00:05:49.808 --> 00:05:54.923
and Service Health dot Read dot All for
the issues and the health overviews.

92
00:05:54.923 --> 00:05:58.222
All three are marked
verified, and nothing writes.

93
00:05:58.222 --> 00:06:03.776
Step two prints each cloud's Graph
host: the worldwide host for G C C with

94
00:06:03.776 --> 00:06:09.520
the Global environment, then the U S
Government hosts for GCC High and DoD.

95
00:06:09.520 --> 00:06:14.307
Step three collects Microsoft
365 from the day-one fixtures,

96
00:06:14.307 --> 00:06:19.573
as of November ninth, 2026: six
notices, five open, two needing action.

97
00:06:19.573 --> 00:06:23.840
Three advisories, a change,
an incident and a retirement.

98
00:06:27.590 --> 00:06:29.540
Step four reads the records.

99
00:06:29.540 --> 00:06:35.634
M C nine hundred thousand and one is
a retirement, of Basic authentication

100
00:06:35.634 --> 00:06:41.028
for S M T P AUTH client submission,
due on January thirty-first, 2027.

101
00:06:41.028 --> 00:06:43.878
M C nine hundred thousand
and three is a change,

102
00:06:43.878 --> 00:06:48.678
an update to SharePoint Framework solutions,
and it needs action with no date.

103
00:06:48.678 --> 00:06:52.727
The incident is open, and the
resolved Teams issue is closed.

104
00:06:52.727 --> 00:06:55.866
Step five asks who said
each one needs action.

105
00:06:55.866 --> 00:06:58.358
For the retirement, it was Microsoft:

106
00:06:58.358 --> 00:07:01.515
the basis is provider,
action-required-by date.

107
00:07:01.515 --> 00:07:06.057
For the SharePoint change, it was the
kit's rule: a prevent-or-fix-issue post

108
00:07:06.057 --> 00:07:10.827
without a date is action required, and
the record says rule, with the category.

109
00:07:10.827 --> 00:07:12.453
Everything else says none.

110
00:07:12.453 --> 00:07:16.285
So when someone asks where a date
came from, the record answers,

111
00:07:16.285 --> 00:07:19.800
and where Microsoft gave
none, the kit makes none up.

112
00:07:23.550 --> 00:07:28.800
Step six collects the second week, as
of November sixteenth: seven notices.

113
00:07:28.800 --> 00:07:32.198
A new post, M C nine
hundred thousand and five,

114
00:07:32.198 --> 00:07:37.200
is a change due February twenty-eighth,
2027, a date Microsoft set.

115
00:07:37.200 --> 00:07:40.465
The SharePoint post's title
now starts with updated,

116
00:07:40.465 --> 00:07:44.262
so its fingerprint changes, and
Module 7 will update its task.

117
00:07:44.262 --> 00:07:45.962
And the incident is closed.

118
00:07:45.962 --> 00:07:49.350
Step seven builds the live
request without sending it.

119
00:07:49.350 --> 00:07:52.928
The first line prints
the messages U R L on the

120
00:07:52.928 --> 00:07:56.313
worldwide Graph host,
the right one for G C C.

121
00:07:56.313 --> 00:08:01.179
Then it tries a next link on another
host, graph dot example dot net,

122
00:08:01.179 --> 00:08:04.874
and the kit refuses it: it
isn't on the G C C Graph host.

123
00:08:04.874 --> 00:08:08.050
That's the guard that keeps the
token on the tenant's own host.

124
00:08:08.050 --> 00:08:12.662
Step eight runs this chapter's
tests: fifteen pass, none fail.

125
00:08:12.662 --> 00:08:14.850
Then delete the output folder.

126
00:08:18.600 --> 00:08:23.331
Lab 2 runs the same read in
a Microsoft 365 test tenant,

127
00:08:23.331 --> 00:08:27.525
in the worldwide or G C C
cloud, never production.

128
00:08:27.525 --> 00:08:31.050
Sign in as a test administrator
who holds an admin role.

129
00:08:31.050 --> 00:08:35.059
Learn doesn't name which admin roles
Graph accepts for a delegated read,

130
00:08:35.059 --> 00:08:38.388
so the Message center reader
roles are the narrowest to try.

131
00:08:38.388 --> 00:08:41.739
Sign in delegated, with
only the two read scopes;

132
00:08:41.739 --> 00:08:44.575
an administrator consents to them once.

133
00:08:44.575 --> 00:08:47.783
Cross-check Learn's own cmdlet
against the kit's collection,

134
00:08:47.783 --> 00:08:49.812
and look at the posts that need action.

135
00:08:49.812 --> 00:08:51.564
Then record one open item:

136
00:08:51.564 --> 00:08:55.737
the tags you see on retirement
posts, which Learn doesn't list.

137
00:08:55.737 --> 00:08:59.676
To back it out, sign out, delete
the run, and remove the consent in

138
00:08:59.676 --> 00:09:04.000
the Microsoft Entra admin center if
it was granted only for this lab.

139
00:09:04.000 --> 00:09:06.150
The authors haven't run Lab 2.

140
00:09:06.150 --> 00:09:09.560
The workbook's Chapter 2
has the exact commands.

141
00:09:13.310 --> 00:09:17.147
The workbook chapter lists six
dangers, and each has a guard.

142
00:09:17.147 --> 00:09:21.686
A GCC High or DoD tenant read on
the worldwide host returns nothing,

143
00:09:21.686 --> 00:09:27.050
and the gate passes on an empty list; the
guard is the cloud named in the settings,

144
00:09:27.050 --> 00:09:31.011
the session guard, and every
request on the cloud's own host.

145
00:09:31.011 --> 00:09:35.919
A next link to another host would send the
token somewhere the agency didn't choose;

146
00:09:35.919 --> 00:09:38.735
next links are followed
only on the cloud's host.

147
00:09:38.735 --> 00:09:43.088
A broad Graph scope for convenience
would let the reader change the tenant;

148
00:09:43.088 --> 00:09:45.897
it holds two read permissions,
declared per call.

149
00:09:45.897 --> 00:09:49.793
A kit rule mistaken for Microsoft's
deadline would give a post

150
00:09:49.793 --> 00:09:53.536
with no date an invented one;
the action basis names the rule.

151
00:09:53.536 --> 00:09:56.555
Data privacy posts go unseen
by the reader's role,

152
00:09:56.555 --> 00:09:59.197
so name a privacy reader
where they matter.

153
00:09:59.197 --> 00:10:01.344
And look-alike email gets acted on,

154
00:10:01.344 --> 00:10:05.254
so the task links the post the kit
read from Graph, as the intake,

155
00:10:05.254 --> 00:10:11.140
triage and routing chapter of Compliance
Automation and Day-Two Operations requires.

156
00:10:14.890 --> 00:10:16.277
The federal requirement.

157
00:10:16.277 --> 00:10:22.925
Gate 2 supports NIST special publication
eight hundred fifty-three S A nine,

158
00:10:22.925 --> 00:10:26.090
part c, and S I five,
part a, as Gate 1 does.

159
00:10:26.090 --> 00:10:30.364
And for posts that announce a change,
it supports C M three, part b:

160
00:10:30.364 --> 00:10:35.377
reviewing proposed changes with a
security and privacy impact analysis,

161
00:10:35.377 --> 00:10:37.678
through the tasks Module 7 routes.

162
00:10:37.678 --> 00:10:43.075
The evidence is the permission list, two
read permissions, and the consent record;

163
00:10:43.075 --> 00:10:47.104
the raw Microsoft 365 response,
the notices and the manifest,

164
00:10:47.104 --> 00:10:50.326
with every request on the
tenant's own Graph host;

165
00:10:50.326 --> 00:10:54.677
and the change and retirement
notices, each with its action basis.

166
00:10:54.677 --> 00:10:57.589
The gate's output is
evidence for these controls;

167
00:10:57.589 --> 00:11:01.884
your organization still sets their
parameters, policy and procedures,

168
00:11:01.884 --> 00:11:05.015
and the assessor decides
whether each is satisfied.

169
00:11:05.015 --> 00:11:09.052
K S I's are FedRAMP twenty X indicators
for cloud service providers,

170
00:11:09.052 --> 00:11:13.360
and which file supports which
indicator is the program's mapping.

171
00:11:17.110 --> 00:11:19.085
Check yourself with two questions.

172
00:11:19.085 --> 00:11:23.322
First: a G C C tenant is
read on which Graph host?

173
00:11:23.322 --> 00:11:28.461
The answer is c: graph dot microsoft
dot com, the worldwide endpoint.

174
00:11:28.461 --> 00:11:34.497
GCC High and DoD have their own hosts,
but G C C keeps the worldwide one.

175
00:11:34.497 --> 00:11:39.560
Second: M C nine hundred thousand and
three is action required with no date.

176
00:11:39.560 --> 00:11:40.248
Why?

177
00:11:40.248 --> 00:11:41.473
The answer is b.

178
00:11:41.473 --> 00:11:44.881
The kit's rule marks a
prevent-or-fix-issue post without

179
00:11:44.881 --> 00:11:48.061
a date as action required,
and labels the basis rule.

180
00:11:48.061 --> 00:11:51.585
Microsoft didn't set a date,
and the kit doesn't make one up.

181
00:11:51.585 --> 00:11:54.012
The module page has three more questions,

182
00:11:54.012 --> 00:11:56.860
each linked to the
section that answers it.

183
00:12:00.610 --> 00:12:01.435
To sum up.

184
00:12:01.435 --> 00:12:05.556
You listed the reader's two permissions,
saw each cloud's Graph host,

185
00:12:05.556 --> 00:12:10.502
collected six posts and issues and then
the second week's seven, told Microsoft's

186
00:12:10.502 --> 00:12:15.298
dates from the kit's rule, and watched the
kit refuse a next link on another host.

187
00:12:15.298 --> 00:12:20.668
The evidence an ISSO keeps for Gate
2 is the raw Microsoft 365 response,

188
00:12:20.668 --> 00:12:26.316
the notices as JSON and as a spreadsheet
file, and the manifest, all dated;

189
00:12:26.316 --> 00:12:31.223
the permission list from step one;
and in Lab 2, the consent record.

190
00:12:31.223 --> 00:12:34.298
Before you ship, work through
the chapter's checklist.

191
00:12:34.298 --> 00:12:38.810
Next is Module 4: Oracle Cloud
Infrastructure announcements.

192
00:12:38.810 --> 00:12:41.768
That's Gate 3, with the
workbook's third chapter:

193
00:12:41.768 --> 00:12:46.360
one policy statement, announcement
chains, and the realms the kit reads.

194
00:12:46.360 --> 00:12:49.480
For an agency whose HR
platform runs on Oracle Cloud,

195
00:12:49.480 --> 00:12:52.380
that's where its maintenance
notices come from.
